{
  "name": "linkedin-poster",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "sticky-readme",
      "name": "Setup Notes",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -260,
        -560
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 700,
        "height": 500,
        "content": "## linkedin-poster\nWebhook-triggered, per-platform posting workflow for a LinkedIn personal profile, using the current LinkedIn **Posts API** (`/rest/posts`, replaces the deprecated UGC Posts API). Credentials come from the request payload only — never stored in n8n (see websitePlan.md §5).\n\nImage/video need LinkedIn's own binary upload flow (initialize → PUT bytes → [video only] finalize), same shape as the youtube-poster Merge pattern: the binary download and the upload-session init are independent HTTP calls, so they run in parallel and a **Merge** node recombines them before the binary PUT.\n\nCreated posts don't return an ID in the response body — LinkedIn puts it in the `x-restli-id` response header, so the publish nodes use Full Response mode.\n\n**Required app setup:** \"Share on LinkedIn\" product, `w_member_social` scope on the access token, and the author must be the token's own profile (`urn:li:person:...`).\n\n**After importing:** activate the workflow, then:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:5678/webhook/linkedin-poster \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\n    \"post_id\": \"pst_test005\",\n    \"user_id\": \"usr_test\",\n    \"platform\": \"linkedin\",\n    \"content\": \"Test post #smposting\",\n    \"media_type\": \"TEXT\",\n    \"media_urls\": [],\n    \"token\": {\n      \"access_token\": \"YOUR_LINKEDIN_ACCESS_TOKEN\",\n      \"author_urn\": \"urn:li:person:3SuMVf0Fy2\"\n    },\n    \"callback_url\": \"https://webhook.site/your-id\"\n  }'\n```\n\n`media_type` accepts `TEXT | IMAGE | VIDEO | CAROUSEL`. `TEXT` ignores `media_urls`; `IMAGE`/`VIDEO` need exactly 1 entry; `CAROUSEL` needs 2-20 entries (all direct hosted URLs — this workflow downloads each and re-uploads the bytes to LinkedIn, since unlike Graph API, LinkedIn won't fetch by URL itself).\n\n**Carousel (multi-image) posts:** uses LinkedIn's `content.multiImage` post type, not `content.carousel` — the Posts API restricts `carousel` to sponsored/ads posts only, while `multiImage` is the organic equivalent (2-20 images, same Images API upload flow repeated once per photo via `Split Carousel URLs`, `Merge Carousel Image Upload`, and `Collect Carousel Image URNs`, mirroring the per-part fan-out pattern used for video). No `finalizeUpload` step for images — the upload PUT alone is enough, same as the single-`IMAGE` branch.\n\n**Video upload — multi-part handling:** LinkedIn's `initializeUpload` returns one `uploadInstructions` entry per byte-range part (files over 4,194,304 bytes get split into multiple parts by LinkedIn itself, not by us). `Split Video Into Parts` (Code node) downloads the file once, then fans it out into one item per part — each with its own byte-range slice as binary data via `this.helpers.getBinaryDataBuffer`/`prepareBinaryData` — so the existing `LI Upload Video Binary` PUT node runs once per part automatically (n8n processes one output per input item). `Collect Video Part ETags` gathers each part's `ETag` response header, in order, into the `uploadedPartIds` array `LI Finalize Video Upload` needs. Works uniformly whether LinkedIn returns 1 part or many.\n\n**Known limitations:** video publish uses a blind 30s Wait instead of polling `GET /rest/videos/{id}` until `status: AVAILABLE`, matching the pattern already used for Threads/Instagram — revisit if publishes start failing with \"media not ready\". `LinkedIn-Version` header is hardcoded to `202609`; LinkedIn versions are supported for a minimum of one year, so bump this periodically per LinkedIn's versioning docs. Malformed source video containers (e.g. bad `.webm` metadata) will still come back `PROCESSING_FAILED`/`CORRUPTED_ENTITY` from LinkedIn — re-encoding is a separate, not-yet-built fix."
      }
    },
